1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,270
Area and volume questions look like
they need a long list of formulas.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:05,127
They don't.

3
00:00:05,147 --> 00:00:07,804
Almost every formula you need
is on the reference sheet,

4
00:00:07,824 --> 00:00:10,340
and you can open it any
time during the test.

5
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,432
What costs points is how
the formulas get used.

6
00:00:13,511 --> 00:00:15,725
A diameter used as a radius.

7
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:19,404
A scale factor used once
instead of three times.

8
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:21,705
Liters mixed with cubic centimeters.

9
00:00:21,725 --> 00:00:23,796
A missing one third on a cone.

10
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:28,205
This lesson fixes all four, then
works through six practice questions,

11
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:29,569
ending with a hard one.

12
00:00:29,589 --> 00:00:31,053
Start with what you're given.

13
00:00:31,232 --> 00:00:35,589
For flat shapes: a rectangle
is length times width.

14
00:00:35,696 --> 00:00:38,981
A triangle is one half base times height.

15
00:00:39,409 --> 00:00:44,589
A circle's area is pi r squared,
and its circumference is two pi r.

16
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:49,728
For solids: a rectangular prism is
length times width times height.

17
00:00:49,978 --> 00:00:52,815
A cylinder is pi r squared h.

18
00:00:52,835 --> 00:00:55,620
A sphere is four thirds pi r cubed.

19
00:00:55,728 --> 00:00:58,278
A cone is one third pi r squared h.

20
00:00:58,298 --> 00:01:01,848
And a pyramid is one third
length times width times height.

21
00:01:01,868 --> 00:01:02,868
There's a pattern.

22
00:01:03,261 --> 00:01:07,975
A prism or a cylinder is the area
of the base times the height.

23
00:01:08,118 --> 00:01:10,205
A cone or a pyramid comes to a point,

24
00:01:10,225 --> 00:01:11,848
so it's one third of that.

25
00:01:11,868 --> 00:01:16,776
A cone holds exactly one third as much
as a cylinder with the same base and

26
00:01:16,796 --> 00:01:17,388
height.

27
00:01:17,408 --> 00:01:18,408
One more habit.

28
00:01:18,479 --> 00:01:21,265
Every circle formula uses the radius.

29
00:01:21,336 --> 00:01:23,209
If a question gives you the diameter,

30
00:01:23,229 --> 00:01:27,584
cut it in half and write the radius on
the figure before you touch a formula.

31
00:01:27,604 --> 00:01:30,905
A sphere with a diameter of
six has a radius of three,

32
00:01:30,925 --> 00:01:34,406
so its volume is four thirds
pi times twenty-seven,

33
00:01:34,426 --> 00:01:36,139
or thirty-six pi.

34
00:01:36,191 --> 00:01:37,814
Two things are not on the reference sheet.

35
00:01:37,834 --> 00:01:39,798
The first is surface area.

36
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,493
Volume is how much fits inside.

37
00:01:42,513 --> 00:01:45,313
Surface area is the total
area of the outside,

38
00:01:45,333 --> 00:01:46,492
like the wrapping paper.

39
00:01:46,512 --> 00:01:51,299
Volume is in cubic units, and
surface area is in square units.

40
00:01:51,370 --> 00:01:53,421
For a box, add up the six faces.

41
00:01:53,441 --> 00:01:58,064
They come in three matching pairs, so
surface area is two times the quantity l w

42
00:01:58,084 --> 00:02:01,313
plus l h plus w h.

43
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:05,369
A box that's four by three by
two has a volume of twenty-four,

44
00:02:05,584 --> 00:02:12,477
but a surface area of fifty-two.

45
00:02:12,631 --> 00:02:15,861
A cylinder unrolls into two
circles and a rectangle.

46
00:02:15,881 --> 00:02:19,253
The rectangle's width is
the circle's circumference.

47
00:02:19,273 --> 00:02:24,329
So the surface area is two
pi r squared plus two pi r h.

48
00:02:24,349 --> 00:02:26,564
The second thing is composite shapes.

49
00:02:26,884 --> 00:02:30,314
When a figure is built from
pieces, add the pieces.

50
00:02:30,635 --> 00:02:33,794
When part of a shape is cut out
and you need the part that's left,

51
00:02:33,814 --> 00:02:34,885
subtract.

52
00:02:35,134 --> 00:02:39,492
Shaded area equals the whole
shape minus what's removed.

53
00:02:39,522 --> 00:02:42,073
Now the rule the SAT loves.

54
00:02:42,093 --> 00:02:45,181
If every length of a
shape is multiplied by k,

55
00:02:45,201 --> 00:02:50,700
the area is multiplied by k squared,
and the volume is multiplied by k cubed.

56
00:02:50,736 --> 00:02:51,736
Here's why.

57
00:02:51,843 --> 00:02:54,644
Double the edge of a
cube, from two to four.

58
00:02:54,664 --> 00:02:57,680
Each face goes from four
square units to sixteen.

59
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:00,037
That's four times as much, two squared.

60
00:03:00,057 --> 00:03:02,573
The volume goes from eight to sixty-four.

61
00:03:02,593 --> 00:03:05,021
That's eight times as much, two cubed.

62
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:06,213
It works in reverse too.

63
00:03:06,233 --> 00:03:09,677
If you know the area ratio,
take the square root to get k.

64
00:03:09,697 --> 00:03:12,463
If you know the volume
ratio, take the cube root.

65
00:03:12,483 --> 00:03:15,820
This only works when
the shapes are similar,

66
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:18,661
meaning every length is
scaled by the same k.

67
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:22,556
On the test, area and volume
questions come in a few types.

68
00:03:22,576 --> 00:03:25,485
Find the area of a
composite or shaded figure.

69
00:03:25,505 --> 00:03:28,021
Work backward from a
volume to a missing length.

70
00:03:28,041 --> 00:03:30,199
Scale a shape and compare.

71
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:31,985
Fill a container at some rate.

72
00:03:32,005 --> 00:03:33,838
Or find a surface area.

73
00:03:33,858 --> 00:03:36,714
A few words are always
worth a second look:

74
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:42,357
diameter, surface area, similar, and
any unit that doesn't match the others.

75
00:03:42,572 --> 00:03:45,072
Those words are where the traps hide.

76
00:03:45,132 --> 00:03:46,183
Question one.

77
00:03:46,203 --> 00:03:50,933
A rectangular metal plate is twenty
inches long and fifteen inches wide.

78
00:03:50,953 --> 00:03:54,040
Two circular holes, each with
a diameter of six inches,

79
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:55,540
are cut out of the plate.

80
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:59,733
What is the area, in square
inches, of the metal that remains?

81
00:03:59,753 --> 00:04:01,038
Pause and try it.

82
00:04:04,274 --> 00:04:07,075
The metal that remains is the
whole plate minus the holes.

83
00:04:07,095 --> 00:04:11,467
The plate is twenty times fifteen,
three hundred square inches.

84
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:12,701
Now the holes.

85
00:04:12,774 --> 00:04:16,774
The diameter is six, so write a
radius of three on the figure.

86
00:04:16,815 --> 00:04:20,492
Each hole is pi times three
squared, which is nine pi.

87
00:04:20,708 --> 00:04:22,509
Two holes make eighteen pi.

88
00:04:22,529 --> 00:04:26,421
So the metal that remains is
three hundred minus eighteen pi.

89
00:04:26,458 --> 00:04:27,815
The answer is C.

90
00:04:27,861 --> 00:04:30,182
A subtracts only one hole.

91
00:04:30,218 --> 00:04:33,484
B uses the circumference, six pi per hole,

92
00:04:33,504 --> 00:04:34,861
instead of the area.

93
00:04:34,932 --> 00:04:40,805
D uses the diameter as the radius: pi
times six squared is thirty-six pi per

94
00:04:40,825 --> 00:04:43,772
hole, so seventy-two pi for two.

95
00:04:43,792 --> 00:04:44,879
Question two.

96
00:04:44,899 --> 00:04:49,542
A cube has a surface area of one
hundred fifty square centimeters.

97
00:04:49,613 --> 00:04:53,078
What is the volume of the
cube, in cubic centimeters?

98
00:04:53,119 --> 00:04:54,405
Pause and try it.

99
00:04:57,640 --> 00:04:59,513
Surface area isn't on the reference sheet,

100
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:00,692
so build it.

101
00:05:00,712 --> 00:05:05,513
A cube has six square faces, and
each face has area s squared.

102
00:05:05,533 --> 00:05:07,869
So six s squared equals one fifty.

103
00:05:07,889 --> 00:05:10,442
Divide by six: s squared is twenty-five,

104
00:05:10,462 --> 00:05:12,311
so each edge is five.

105
00:05:12,331 --> 00:05:13,510
Now the volume.

106
00:05:13,724 --> 00:05:18,311
A cube's volume is s cubed, and five
cubed is one hundred twenty-five.

107
00:05:18,331 --> 00:05:20,009
Enter one twenty-five.

108
00:05:20,846 --> 00:05:24,826
Twenty-five is the area of one
face, and five is the edge.

109
00:05:24,846 --> 00:05:27,219
Both show up on the way to the answer.

110
00:05:27,239 --> 00:05:30,811
Check that you're giving the
volume, not an area or a length.

111
00:05:30,867 --> 00:05:32,188
Question three.

112
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:37,811
A right circular cone has a volume of
ninety-six pi cubic inches and a height of

113
00:05:37,831 --> 00:05:38,938
eight inches.

114
00:05:38,973 --> 00:05:41,774
What is the radius of
the base of the cone,

115
00:05:41,794 --> 00:05:42,866
in inches?

116
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:44,205
Pause and try it.

117
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:50,884
Start from the formula
and plug in what you know.

118
00:05:50,904 --> 00:05:54,170
One third pi r squared
h equals ninety-six pi,

119
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:54,920
with h equal to eight.

120
00:05:54,940 --> 00:06:00,027
Divide both sides by pi, then
multiply both sides by three.

121
00:06:00,047 --> 00:06:03,548
That gives eight r squared
equals two hundred eighty-eight.

122
00:06:03,735 --> 00:06:06,537
Divide by eight: r squared is thirty-six,

123
00:06:06,557 --> 00:06:08,557
so the radius is six.

124
00:06:08,664 --> 00:06:10,464
The answer is B.

125
00:06:10,484 --> 00:06:13,642
A is what you get if you
forget the one third:

126
00:06:13,662 --> 00:06:18,304
eight r squared equals ninety-six,
so r is the square root of twelve.

127
00:06:18,341 --> 00:06:19,000
C is the diameter.

128
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:21,571
D is r squared.

129
00:06:21,591 --> 00:06:24,408
Cones and pyramids
always get the one third.

130
00:06:24,428 --> 00:06:25,607
Question four.

131
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,658
An aquarium shaped like a rectangular
prism is fifty centimeters long,

132
00:06:30,678 --> 00:06:33,963
thirty centimeters wide,
and forty centimeters tall.

133
00:06:34,071 --> 00:06:38,285
It starts empty, and water is poured
in at three liters per minute.

134
00:06:38,357 --> 00:06:41,917
One liter equals one
thousand cubic centimeters.

135
00:06:41,937 --> 00:06:46,310
How many minutes will it take for the
water to reach a depth of twenty-four

136
00:06:46,330 --> 00:06:47,330
centimeters?

137
00:06:47,393 --> 00:06:48,678
Pause and try it.

138
00:06:52,913 --> 00:06:54,358
The water forms its own box.

139
00:06:54,378 --> 00:06:56,965
It has the same length
and width as the tank,

140
00:06:56,985 --> 00:06:59,251
but its height is the depth, twenty-four,

141
00:06:59,271 --> 00:07:00,178
not forty.

142
00:07:00,198 --> 00:07:04,913
Fifty times thirty times twenty-four is
thirty-six thousand cubic centimeters.

143
00:07:04,954 --> 00:07:06,113
Now match the units.

144
00:07:06,133 --> 00:07:08,398
The rate is in liters, so convert.

145
00:07:08,418 --> 00:07:11,792
Thirty-six thousand cubic
centimeters is thirty-six liters.

146
00:07:11,812 --> 00:07:14,792
At three liters per minute,
that takes twelve minutes.

147
00:07:14,812 --> 00:07:16,418
The answer is A.

148
00:07:16,465 --> 00:07:18,501
B fills the whole tank,
all forty centimeters.

149
00:07:18,644 --> 00:07:21,374
C is the volume in liters, not the time.

150
00:07:21,394 --> 00:07:27,195
D divides thirty-six thousand
by three without converting.

151
00:07:27,215 --> 00:07:29,680
Units have to match before you divide.

152
00:07:29,750 --> 00:07:30,944
Question five.

153
00:07:30,964 --> 00:07:35,659
The radius and the height of a large
cylindrical can are each one point five

154
00:07:35,679 --> 00:07:39,301
times the radius and the height
of a small cylindrical can.

155
00:07:39,321 --> 00:07:42,372
The small can holds sixteen fluid ounces.

156
00:07:42,392 --> 00:07:45,286
How many fluid ounces
does the large can hold?

157
00:07:45,343 --> 00:07:46,629
Pause and try it.

158
00:07:49,864 --> 00:07:52,201
Every length is multiplied
by one point five,

159
00:07:52,221 --> 00:07:54,201
so k is one point five.

160
00:07:54,221 --> 00:07:56,415
Volume scales by k cubed.

161
00:07:56,435 --> 00:07:59,522
One point five cubed is
three point three seven five,

162
00:07:59,542 --> 00:08:02,201
and sixteen times that is fifty-four.

163
00:08:02,221 --> 00:08:03,720
The answer is C.

164
00:08:03,841 --> 00:08:05,413
You can see it in the formula.

165
00:08:05,484 --> 00:08:09,750
Pi r squared h has the radius
in it twice and the height once.

166
00:08:09,770 --> 00:08:12,091
That's three factors of one point five.

167
00:08:12,156 --> 00:08:16,457
A scales linearly: sixteen
times one point five.

168
00:08:16,477 --> 00:08:18,870
B uses k squared, the area rule.

169
00:08:18,977 --> 00:08:22,691
D multiplies by three times one
point five instead of cubing.

170
00:08:22,711 --> 00:08:26,298
Before the hardest one, here
are the four traps in one place.

171
00:08:26,318 --> 00:08:30,032
Diameter used as radius:
cut it in half first.

172
00:08:30,138 --> 00:08:33,191
Scaling linearly: area gets k squared,

173
00:08:33,211 --> 00:08:35,067
and volume gets k cubed.

174
00:08:35,109 --> 00:08:37,624
Mixed units: convert before you calculate.

175
00:08:37,644 --> 00:08:40,339
And the missing one third:
anything that comes to a point,

176
00:08:40,359 --> 00:08:43,463
like a cone or a pyramid, gets one third.

177
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:45,284
Question six, the hardest one.

178
00:08:45,304 --> 00:08:47,820
Pyramid P and pyramid Q are similar.

179
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:51,427
The surface area of pyramid P
is seventy-two square inches,

180
00:08:51,447 --> 00:08:55,569
and the surface area of pyramid Q is
one hundred sixty-two square inches.

181
00:08:55,589 --> 00:08:58,802
The volume of pyramid P
is forty cubic inches.

182
00:08:58,822 --> 00:09:02,036
What is the volume of
pyramid Q, in cubic inches?

183
00:09:02,150 --> 00:09:03,237
Pause and try it.

184
00:09:03,257 --> 00:09:04,828
Take a little longer on this one.

185
00:09:09,027 --> 00:09:11,848
You're given areas, but
you're asked about volume.

186
00:09:12,062 --> 00:09:13,079
So go through length.

187
00:09:13,099 --> 00:09:16,742
The area ratio is one
sixty-two over seventy-two.

188
00:09:17,027 --> 00:09:20,562
Divide both by eighteen,
and that's nine fourths.

189
00:09:20,623 --> 00:09:21,210
That's k squared.

190
00:09:21,230 --> 00:09:23,924
Take the square root,
and k is three halves.

191
00:09:23,944 --> 00:09:29,782
Every length of pyramid Q is one and a
half times the matching length of pyramid

192
00:09:29,802 --> 00:09:30,432
P.

193
00:09:30,452 --> 00:09:32,361
Volume scales by k cubed.

194
00:09:32,381 --> 00:09:35,039
Three halves cubed is
twenty-seven eighths.

195
00:09:35,059 --> 00:09:39,004
Forty times twenty-seven eighths:
forty divided by eight is five,

196
00:09:39,024 --> 00:09:41,932
and five times twenty-seven
is one hundred thirty-five.

197
00:09:41,952 --> 00:09:43,917
Enter one thirty-five.

198
00:09:44,034 --> 00:09:48,442
If you got ninety, you scaled
the volume by the area ratio.

199
00:09:48,462 --> 00:09:51,978
If you got sixty, you
used the length ratio.

200
00:09:51,998 --> 00:09:55,227
Areas to lengths to volumes: square root,

201
00:09:55,247 --> 00:09:56,247
then cube.

202
00:09:56,290 --> 00:09:59,949
Here's the method for any
area or volume question.

203
00:09:59,969 --> 00:10:02,485
One: label the figure.

204
00:10:02,505 --> 00:10:05,897
Write the radius, not the
diameter, and mark what's given.

205
00:10:06,005 --> 00:10:07,948
Two: find the formula.

206
00:10:07,968 --> 00:10:09,520
It's probably on the reference sheet,

207
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:12,040
with one third for cones and pyramids.

208
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:15,002
Three: match the units
before you calculate.

209
00:10:15,022 --> 00:10:18,646
Four: if a shape is scaled, ask
whether you're scaling a length,

210
00:10:18,666 --> 00:10:20,594
an area, or a volume.

211
00:10:20,630 --> 00:10:23,252
Five: reread the last line.

212
00:10:23,272 --> 00:10:25,146
Surface area or volume?

213
00:10:25,166 --> 00:10:26,896
Radius or diameter?

214
00:10:26,916 --> 00:10:28,166
Depth or full?

215
00:10:28,202 --> 00:10:32,324
For more practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter for

216
00:10:32,344 --> 00:10:36,915
area and volume under
Geometry and Trigonometry.

217
00:10:36,987 --> 00:10:40,717
Every question in this video
was written for this video,

218
00:10:40,737 --> 00:10:43,317
in the style of the real test.

219
00:10:43,337 --> 00:10:46,837
Next up: lines, angles, and triangles.

220
00:10:46,909 --> 00:10:50,317
Parallel lines, angle chasing,
and similar triangles.

221
00:10:50,337 --> 00:10:52,623
If this helped, subscribe so you catch it.
